```java
double totalArea(List<Object> shapes) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Object s : shapes) {
        if (s instanceof Circle c) total += Math.PI * c.radius * c.radius;
        else if (s instanceof Square sq) total += sq.side * sq.side;
        else if (s instanceof Triangle t) total += 0.5 * t.base * t.height;
    }
    return total;
}
```

Every caller that needs a shape's area has to repeat this same `instanceof` chain. Add a `Pentagon`, and every one of those call sites needs a new `else if` branch, or it silently contributes zero to the total.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;
    double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
}

double totalArea(List<Shape> shapes) {
    double total = 0;
    for (Shape s : shapes) total += s.area(); // one call site, no branching
    return total;
}
```

The call site (`s.area()`) never changes, no matter how many `Shape` subtypes exist. Each subtype owns its own formula, and the loop doesn't need to know or care which one it's looking at.
